1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Bb4 5. e5 h6 6. Bd2 Bxc3 7. bxc3 Ne4 8. Qg4
g6 9. h4 c5 10. Bd3 Nxd2 11. Kxd2 Qa5 12. Rh3 Nc6 13. Bxg6 Nxd4 $3 {TN; the
tragedy of this game is that I lost after finding a huge improvement on
Bogoljubow's play at the board. At least I won two CC games with it many years
later.} (13... Rf8 $2 {defeatist} 14. Rf3 cxd4 (14... Nxd4 15. Rxf7 Rxf7 16.
Qh5 $1) 15. Bxf7+ Kd8 16. Qg7 dxc3+ 17. Ke1 Qb4 18. Bg6 Rxf3 19. Nxf3 Qf4 20.
Rd1 Rb8 21. Qf6+ $18 {Euwe-Bogoljubow, Budapest 1921}) (13... Rg8 $2 {loses to}
14. Bxf7+ Kxf7 15. Rf3+ {But my N on d4 covers f3 preventing this trick.}) 14.
Rg3 $5 (14. Bh7 Bd7 15. Ne2 O-O-O $13 {0-1,26 Schmidt,Hans-Harding, ICCF EU/M/
GT/360 corr 1992}) (14. Kd1 Rg8 15. Bxf7+ Kxf7 16. Qh5+ Ke7 17. cxd4 Rxg2 18.
Ne2 Bd7 $1 {Originally I had thought 18...Rxf2 (which my opponent perhaps
expected) but fortunately in 1984 CC-GM Dougie Bryson showed me that White can
reply 19 Rf3! and he suggested Bd7 instead.} 19. Rf3 $2 ({Stockfish shows that
White may be OK with} 19. Qf3) 19... Rag8 $19 {Granski,M-Harding, 5th European
Team Ch corr 1995; the move I played next was not in the engine's top three
but I won eventually.}) 14... Rf8 15. Bh7 Bd7 16. Qf4 O-O-O 17. Qxh6 Nb5 $6 {
Loses most of Black's advantage. There are several better moves.} (17... Bb5
$143 $17 {probably best}) 18. Ne2 Bc6 $6 {Too slow} (18... d4) (18... Nxc3) 19.
Ke1 d4 20. Kf1 Nxc3 (20... Rh8 $142) 21. Nxc3 dxc3 22. h5 $13 Qb4 23. Kg1 Qd4
$2 (23... Qh4 {is one of several better moves}) 24. Re1 (24. Qg7 $142) 24...
Qd2 25. Qe3 Qxe3 $4 {Terrible decision, finally spoiling the game} (25... Rh8
$15) 26. Rexe3 Rd1+ 27. Kh2 Rd2 28. h6 $1 Rxf2 29. Bg8 $1 $18 f5 30. exf6 R2xf6
$2 $138 (30... R8xf6 {but it is lost anyway}) 31. h7 Rh6+ 32. Rh3 Rff6 33. Rxe6
Rxh7 34. Rxh7 Rf2 35. Rg6 a5 (35... Be4 36. Be6+) 36. Rxc6+ bxc6 37. Bb3 c4 38.
Ba4 $18 c5 39. Ra7 Rf1 40. g4 Ra1 41. g5 Kb8 42. Rf7 Rb1 43. g6 Rb6 44. g7 Rh6+
45. Kg3 Rg6+ 46. Kf4 Rg1 47. Ke5 Rg6 $2 {No doubt time was very short or I
could have resigned.} 48. Rf8+ Kc7 49. g8=Q Rxg8 50. Rxg8 Kb6 51. Kd6 Ka6 52.
Kc6 Ka7 53. Kc7 1-0

