1. Nf3 Nf6 2. g3 g6 3. d4 Bg7 4. c4 O-O 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O Nbd7 7. Nc3 e5 8. e4
c6 9. b3 ({This unusual variation has the virtue of not entering lines that
are more popular, like} 9. h3 {or 9 Rb1, that lately have been practised a lot
and are well known by all different analysis. I believe, however, that 9 b3
has a special quality - that of SURPRISE!}) 9... exd4 10. Nxd4 Re8 11. h3 Nc5
12. Re1 d5 $2 ({My young opponent, before playing this last move, thought much
time about it and was tempted to refute 9 b3 over the board instead of
continung with} 12... a5 {, which is the regular move in this scheme. Kinlay
must have thought that if the centre disappeared, he would be able to achieve
equality, but in this situation the advantage goes to the one who takes the
initiative.}) 13. cxd5 cxd5 14. exd5 Rxe1+ 15. Qxe1 Nxd5 16. Nxd5 Bxd4 17. Be3
$1 {With this, White has the advantage.} Bxa1 ({If} 17... Nd3 18. Qf1 $1 Bxe3
19. Nxe3 {and White's maintain the initiative.}) 18. Qxa1 Nd7 19. Bd4 $1 {
Positionally won by White because his bishop controls this tremendous diagonal
that permits him to realise many combinations.} a5 20. Qc3 f6 21. Nc7 Rb8 22.
Bd5+ Kh8 23. Ne8 $1 Ne5 24. Nxf6 {Source: Quinteros notes in the bulletin;
CHESS, October 1977, Vol.43 Nos.779-780, p15} 1-0

