1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Be7 6. dxe5 ({An alternative
to the more usual} 6. Qe2) 6... d5 ({Formanek is following a game he had as
White against Pachman when the grandmaster gained a good position with this
move. A more solid possibility is} 6... O-O) 7. c4 a6 ({Smejkal-Smyslov,
Hastings 1971/72, went} 7... dxc4 8. Nd4 Bd7 9. Nxc6 bxc6 10. Bxc4 {with a
clear plus for White.}) 8. Ba4 dxc4 9. Nd4 Nc5 10. Bxc6+ bxc6 11. Be3 {White
reserves the option of moving the b1 N to c3 after 11...Qd5 or of going to a3
or d2 to pick up the c4-pawn.} ({Now Formanek played} 11. Nxc6 Qxd1 12. Rxd1 {
against Pachman but after} Nd3 {Black is certainly no worse. (The reference is
to Formanek-Pachman, Reggio Emilia 1975/76, 0-1, 40 moves - JS)}) 11... Bb7 ({
What else?} 11... Rb8 {loses material}) ({and} 11... O-O {is horrible after}
12. Nxc6) ({If} 11... Bd7 12. Nd2 {is possible as the Q can no longer go to d5,
}) ({Perhaps the only real alternative is} 11... a5 12. Nxc6 Qxd1 13. Rxd1 Nd3
{although after} 14. b3 Be6 15. Nd2 {White seems to have an advantage.}) 12.
Nf5 $1 {Threatening to win a piece with Qxd8+.} Qxd1 13. Rxd1 Bf8 ({After} 
13... Nd3 14. Nxg7+ Kd7 (14... Kf8 15. Bh6 Kg8 16. Nd2 $1 {so that if} Nxb2 17.
Ne4 {with a mating attack}) 15. b3 Kc8 16. Nf5 Bd8 17. bxc4 Nxe5 18. Bd4 f6 19.
Nd2 {with a large plus.}) 14. Nd2 g6 15. Nd4 c3 16. bxc3 Be7 ({Black didn't
like the look of} 16... Na4 17. Ne4 O-O-O ({note that in this line} 17... Be7 {
is answered by} 18. Nb3 {occupying c5}) 18. Bg5 {but maybe he could have clung
on with} Rd7) 17. Nc4 {Now White is winning. The threat is Na5 picking up the
c6-pawn.} a5 18. Nb3 {If Black exchanges on b3 he simply loses the a5-pawn, so
he gives up a pawn to get the b7 bishop out.} Na4 19. Ncxa5 Ba6 20. Nxc6 Nxc3
21. Rd2 {It is not so much the extra pawn as Black's crippling weaknesses on
the dark squares which contribute to his speedy defeat.} Ba3 22. Bh6 $1 Bb5 ({
After} 22... Bf8 23. Bxf8 Kxf8 24. Rd7 Bb5 25. Nbd4 Bxc6 26. Nxc6 Rxa2 27. Rxa2
Nxa2 28. Rxc7 {White wins easily.}) 23. Nbd4 Ba4 24. Re1 {Black is further
handicapped by the terrible position of his king. With his next move he tries
to get the h8 rook out with ...g5 and ...Rg6.} Rg8 25. Re3 ({Perhaps an
inaccuracy. Simply} 25. h4 {would keep Black totally tied up.}) 25... Nd5 $2 ({
A blunder. After} 25... Nb1 {(the only move)} 26. Rdd3 Bc1 27. Rh3 Bxh6 28.
Rxh6 Ra6 {Black can continue to offer resistance although White should still
win without trouble, e.g.} 29. Nb4 {heading for d5 and f6.}) 26. Rxa3 Bxc6 27.
Nxc6 Rxa3 28. Rxd5 f5 ({After} 28... Ra8 29. h4 {Black has few moves indeed!})
29. Rd8+ Kf7 30. e6+ {White comes out two pieces up. Black certainly needs a
big improvement if 6...d5 is to be rendered possible. (Sources: Nunn's notes
in the bulletin; CHESS, October 1977, Vol.43 Nos. 779-780, p15)} 1-0

