[EventType "swiss"]
[EventRounds "10"]
[EventCountry "ENG"]

1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 e6 3. e4 ({Avoiding Nunn's intended Tarrasch} 3. d4 c5) 3...
d5 ({The probably better alternative is} 3... c5 4. e5 Ng8 {when} 5. d4 ({
but the gambit} 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. d4 cxd4 7. Nxd4 Nxe5 8. Ndb5 {is keenly debated})
5... cxd4 6. Qxd4 Nc6 7. Qe4 f5 8. Qe2 Bb4 {gives Black an easy game}) 4. e5
Nfd7 $2 (4... Ne4 {is the critical move.}) (4... d4 5. exf6 dxc3 6. bxc3 Qxf6
7. d4 c5 8. Nf3 {favoured White in two games from Wijk aan Zee 1976.
Dvoretsky-Langeweg continued} h6 ({while Tal-Sosonko went} 8... cxd4 9. Bg5 Qf5
({maybe} 9... Qg6 {is better}) 10. cxd4 Bb4+ 11. Bd2 Nc6 12. Bxb4 Nxb4 13. Rb1
Nc6 ({In a later game, Miles-Sosonko, Tilburg 1977, Sosonko tried to improve by
} 13... Qa5 14. Qd2 Nc6 15. Bd3 Qxd2+ 16. Kxd2 b6 {but after} 17. Rhc1 Ba6 18.
Be4 Bb7 19. c5 {White won quickly.}) 14. Bd3 Qf6 15. Be4) 9. Bd3 Bd6 10. O-O
O-O 11. Qe2 Rd8 $2 12. Qe4 Qf5 13. Qh4 Qf6 14. Qxf6 gxf6 15. Bxh6) 5. cxd5 exd5
6. d4 c5 7. Nf3 Nc6 8. Bb5 $1 {The same strong move with which Smyslov beat
Farago at Hastings 1976/77. I could not remember the Smyslov game exactly, but
was confident that Black was in difficulties.} cxd4 ({The Hastings game
finished} 8... a6 9. Bxc6 bxc6 10. O-O Be7 11. dxc5 Nxc5 12. Nd4 Qb6 13. Be3
O-O 14. Rc1 Rd8 15. b4 Ne4 16. Na4 Qb7 17. Nxc6 Re8 {1-0}) 9. Nxd4 Ndxe5 10.
Qe2 $1 {Smyslov mentioned this in his analysis to the Farago game.} Qe7 (10...
f6 {now fails to} 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. Bxc6+ {, so Black;'s desperate answer is
forced.}) 11. Nxd5 Nd3+ 12. Bxd3 Nxd4 13. Qxe7+ ({Avoiding Black's last trap:} 
13. Nxe7 Nxe2 14. Nxc8 Nd4 $1 15. Be3 Nc6 16. Bf5 g6 17. Bh3 f5 18. Nxa7 Bb4+ {
with counterplay.}) 13... Bxe7 14. Nc7+ Kd8 15. Nxa8 Bb4+ 16. Bd2 Re8+ 17. Kd1
Bxd2 ({Or} 17... Bg4+ 18. f3 Nxf3 19. gxf3 {and wins.}) 18. Kxd2 {White wins
easily on material. (Sources: Quinteros's notes in the bulletin; CHESS,
October 1977, Vol.43 Nos.779-780, p16. 250 pounds first prize and Lloyds Bank
Trophy for Quinteros)} 1-0

